\intbl\line
\ql\sb120\sa120\li240\ri0\fi0 Most of the Sun\u8217?s helium has been produced from the Sun\u8217?s hydrogen by\line
the process of nuclear fusion. This process also produces vast quantities\line
of energy. The process takes place in the core of the Sun at a temperature\line
of about 15 million \'b0C and has been going on for about 4.5 billion years.\line
During this period of time, the Sun has remained stable and scientists think\line
that it will remain stable for several billion years into the future.
\intbl \cell\pard\intbl\row\pard
\pard
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a) \~\~\~\~Explain why the Sun remains stable.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (3)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~A scientific opinion is expressed on this web page.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Identify this opinion and suggest how scientists could justify it.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (2)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q31.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The process of nuclear fusion results in the release of energy.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~\~(i)\~\~\~\~\~Describe the process of nuclear fusion.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Where does nuclear fusion happen naturally?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~For many years, scientists have tried to produce a controlled nuclear fusion reaction that lasts long enough to be useful. However, the experimental fusion reactors use more energy than they produce.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ From the information given, suggest \b one\b0  reason why nuclear fusion reactors are not used to produce energy in a nuclear power station.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Suggest \b one\b0  reason why scientists continue to try to develop a practical nuclear fusion reactor.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q32.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Our star, the Sun, is stable.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Explain what the conditions need to be for a star to remain stable.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Shortly after the \u8216?big bang\u8217?, hydrogen was the only element in the Universe.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Explain how the other elements came to be formed.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (3)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q33.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~\~Uranium atoms do not always have the same number of neutrons.\line
What are atoms of the same element that have different numbers of neutrons called?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
