\qc\sb240\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0{\pict\pngblip\picw6615\pich2205\picwgoal6615\pichgoal2205\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Explain what is meant by the term \i black hole\i0 .\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ What is produced as the gases from a star spiral into a black hole?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 6 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q47.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Uranium-234 (\fs20\super 234\fs22\nosupersub U) is a radioactive element. The graph shows the number of protons and neutrons in the nuclei of the elements formed when uranium-234 decays.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw4890\pich3990\picwgoal4890\pichgoal3990\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ How does the graph show that uranium-234 (\fs20\super 234\fs22\nosupersub U) and thorium-230 (\fs20\super 230\fs22\nosupersub Th) emit alpha particles?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ What makes uranium and thorium different elements?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ Radioactive decay may also produce gamma radiation. \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi0 Why does the emission of gamma radiation \b not\f41  \f1\b0 cause a new element to be formed?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ The graph shows how the thickness of different materials needed to absorb 90% of the gamma radiation emitted by a source depends on the energy of the radiation. The energy of the gamma radiation is given in units called electron-volts.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw6855\pich5880\picwgoal6855\pichgoal5880\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Which of the materials shown is least effective at absorbing gamma radiation? \line
Use the information in the graph to give a reason for your answer.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ For gamma radiation of energy 1.5 million electron-volts, how many times more effective is steel than water at absorbing the radiation? Show clearly how you obtain your answer.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ Scientists in the early twentieth century thought that atoms were made up of electrons scattered inside a ball of positive charge. This was called the \u8216?plum-pudding\u8217? model of the atom.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw3585\pich2010\picwgoal3585\pichgoal2010\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Plum pudding model\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567 Rutherford and Marsden did an experiment, in which a beam of alpha particles was aimed at a thin sheet of gold.\line
Explain how the results of this experiment led to a new model of the atom. \line
You may include one or more diagrams in your answer.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (3)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 9 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q48.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Most of the Sun is hydrogen. Inside the core of the sun, hydrogen is being converted to helium. What name is given to this process and why is the process so important?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Describe what will happen to the Sun as the core runs out of hydrogen.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (3)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q49.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The first commercial nuclear power station in the world was built at Calder Hall in Cumbria.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ The fuel used at the Calder Hall power station is uranium. Natural uranium consists mainly of two isotopes: uranium-235{\pict\pngblip\picw615\pich360\picwgoal615\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~and uranium-238{\pict\pngblip\picw615\pich360\picwgoal615\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
}. The nucleus of a uranium-235 atom is different to that of a uranium-238 atom.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Where is the nucleus in an atom?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Name the \b two\b0  types of particle found in the nucleus.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ How is the nucleus of a uranium-238 atom different to the nucleus of a uranium-235 atom?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ In the nuclear reactor fission of uranium atoms takes place in reactions such as the one shown below.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich360\picwgoal480\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ +\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ {\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich360\picwgoal300\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~{\pict\pngblip\picw585\pich195\picwgoal585\pichgoal195\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~{\pict\pngblip\picw375\pich360\picwgoal375\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\~\~+\~\~ {\pict\pngblip\picw420\pich360\picwgoal420\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\~\~+\~\~ 3({\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich360\picwgoal300\pichgoal360\picscalex100\picscaley100
})\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 The nuclear reactions are carefully controlled in the power station so that a chain reaction takes place.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567 Explain, as fully as you can:\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ how fission of uranium atoms takes place in a nuclear reactor;\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ how this leads to a chain reaction;\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ why it can be used to generate electricity.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (4)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 9 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q50.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The first commercial nuclear power station in the world was built at Calder Hall in Cumbria.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567 The atoms produced by the fission of uranium are also radioactive. The used fuel is sent to a reprocessing plant where it can be safely treated.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Calder Hall power station is next to the Sellafield reprocessing plant. Suggest an advantage of having the two plants close together.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~ One of the radioactive products is iodine-138. This has a half-life of 6 seconds.\line
A sample of radioactive material contains 2000 atoms of iodine-138.\line
How long will it take for the number of iodine-131 atoms to decrease to 125?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (3)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 4 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q51.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Nuclear fusion in the Sun releases large amounts of energy.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Explain what is meant by nuclear fusion.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (3)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~ Why is energy released by such nuclear fusion reactions?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q52.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ The Sun is at the stable stage of its life.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Explain, in terms of the forces acting on the Sun, what this means.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (3)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ At the end of the stable stage of its life a star will change.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Describe and explain the changes that could take place.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (6)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 9 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q53.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Our Sun is just one of many millions of stars in a galaxy called the Milky Way.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567 Our Sun is in the main stable period of a star\u8217?s lifetime. The massive force of gravity draws its matter together. This force is balanced by the very high temperatures, from the fusion of hydrogen atoms, which tend to make the Sun expand. Describe and explain what will happen to the Sun as the hydrogen is eventually used up.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (Total 3 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q54.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Studying stars gives scientists evidence about the evolution of the Universe.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ In astronomy, what is meant by a black hole?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ How is it possible to detect a black hole?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ The changes which happen in stars result in new elements being formed.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Nuclei of the heaviest elements are found in the Sun.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567 Describe how these nuclei are formed.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (2)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 6 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q55.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 One theory of the origin of the Universe was that billions of years ago all matter was in one place, then it exploded (\u8216?big bang\u8217?).\line
Describe, in as much detail as you can, how our star (the Sun) formed from the time when there was just dust and gas (mostly hydrogen) up to now when it is in its main stable period.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\i\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567 To gain full marks in this question you should write your ideas in good English. Put them into a sensible order and use the correct scientific words.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\i0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q56.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The Sun at the centre of our solar system is a star.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw4740\pich2880\picwgoal4740\pichgoal2880\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ The Sun contains nuclei of the heaviest elements. Atoms of these heaviest elements are also present in the planets of the solar system. What does this suggest about the material from which the solar system is formed?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (1)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Stars form from gas (mostly hydrogen) and dust.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw7920\pich2355\picwgoal7920\pichgoal2355\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ Describe, in as much detail as you can, what forces allow a stable star to exist and how the star may eventually form a black hole.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ \i To gain full marks in this question you should write your ideas in good English. Put them into a sensible order and use the correct scientific words.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\i0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (6)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 7 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q57.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The flowchart shows four stages thought to occur in the evolution of a star such as our Sun.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw6945\pich4905\picwgoal6945\pichgoal4905\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi0 At a particular time a star might have reached one of these stages or be between stages or be at a further stage. What period in its evolution has our star, the Sun, reached?\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (Total 1 mark)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q58.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 At the very high temperatures in the sun, hydrogen is converted into helium. It takes four hydrogen nuclei to produce one helium nucleus.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567 The table shows the relative masses of hydrogen and helium nuclei.\par
\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567{\pict\pngblip\picw6255\pich1755\picwgoal6255\pichgoal1755\picscalex100\picscaley100
}\~\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Use these figures to calculate what happens to the mass of the sun as hydrogen is converted to helium.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (3)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Use your answer to part (a) to explain how the sun has been able to radiate huge amounts of energy for billions of years.\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0\fi0 (2)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0 (Total 5 marks)\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q59.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The energy radiated by a \b main sequence\b0  star like the Sun is released by a nuclear fusion reaction in its core.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567 Read the following information about this reaction then use it to answer the questions below. \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb120\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ The net result of the nuclear fusion reaction is that four hydrogen nuclei produce one helium nucleus. There is a loss of mass of 0.7%.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb120\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ For nuclear fusion to occur nuclei must collide at very high speeds.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb120\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ The energy released during the reaction can be calculated as shown:\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 energy released [J] = loss of mass [kg]\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ \'d7\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ (speed of light [m/s\'b2]) \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (The speed of light is 3 \'d7 10\fs20\super 8\fs22\nosupersub  m/s)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Calculate the energy released when 1g of hydrogen fuses to form helium. \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 (\i Show your working\i0 .)\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 (4)\par
