\f1\fs27\cf1\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q60.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 ideas that\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ formed from dust/gases\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ pulled together by gravity\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ massive so very large gravitational forces (pulling inwards)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ hydrogen \u8594? helium / fusion releases energy [\ul not\ul0  fission or just \u8216?nuclear\u8217?]\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ high temperature creates high pressure (pushing outwards)\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ long period when forces balance\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ then expands \u8594? red giant / red star\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \u8226?\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ then contracts to (dense) white dwarf / white star\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\i\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567\fi0 [\ul credit\ul0  if massive enough / more massive than sun, red giant \u8594? supernova \u8594? (very dense) neutron star but do not accept w.r.t. Sun itself]\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 [The whole of the (non bracketed part of) each idea \ul must\ul0  be present in some appropriate for in of words for each mark to be credited. To gain more than a single mark ideas must also be in correct sequence and/or appropriately related.]\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb60\sa0\li2268\ri1701 any six 1 mark each\par
\f1\fs20\cf0\b\i0\ltrpar\qr\sb60\sa0\li0\ri0 [6]\par

\pard\sect
\f1\fs27\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb100\sa100 Examiner reports\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q1.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~Just over 80 \'25 of the students scored 2 marks for correctly describing what is meant by an isotope of an element. The number of electrons was ignored as this does not define an isotope.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~Students needed to say light / lighter / small nuclei to demonstrate they understood that it is only lighter nuclei which fuse. The word fuse could have been substituted for other words like \u8216?join\u8217?, or \u8216?combine\u8217? but \u8216?collide\u8217? was insufficient. Some students demonstrated an excellent understanding of the fusion process and fusion in stars was accurately described. Fusion on earth where an extra neutron is released was ignored, as the process as stated in the specification was being tested. Just fewer than 20 \'25 of the students scored 3 marks, while nearly 30 \'25 of the students scored 2 marks and approximately 15 \'25 of the students scored 1 mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~Most students who scored a mark did so for stating that the reactor walls should not melt. Few students scored the first marking point. Most students were vague and used phrases like the walls need to \u8216?withstand the high temperatures\u8217? which did not score a mark. Many students confused specific heat capacity with thermal conductivity and thermal insulation. Containing the fusion energy or products was another common insufficient response. Only about 4 \'25 of the students scored 2 marks, while approximately 40 \'25 of the students scored 1 mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\u8195?\~Just over 30 \'25 of the students scored the mark for the consequence of an uncontrolled chain reaction. \u8216?Explosion\u8217? unqualified was insufficient to score the mark.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q2.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Foundation\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~15 % of students scored 3 marks, 39% scored 2 marks and 30% scored 1 mark. The most common mark to score was either \u8216?nuclei\u8217? or \u8216?gamma rays\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~In this exam series all equations were provided for the students on the Physics Equations Sheet. 94% of students scored the mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~67% of students scored 2 marks for multiplying the power by the time, whether they had converted the power or not. Most attempts to convert the power were incorrect. Only 3% of students scored all 3 marks for this calculation of energy.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\u8195?\~13% of students scored a mark. \u8216?Wear protective clothing\u8217? or \u8216?hazmat suits\u8217? were common incorrect answers. Any idea of burying the radioactive waste was credited, so \u8216?bury it in a field\u8217? was ok, as was \u8216?bury it in landfill\u8217?, which from a student\u8217?s perspective is equivalent to burying it underground. \u8216?Keeping it away from people\u8217? was insufficient.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa360\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (e)\u8195?\~74% of students scored 2 marks, while 3% scored 1 mark. The answer of 29.2 days was worth 1 mark as this could only have been calculated by doing the correct calculation and then subtracting the result from 365.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi0 Higher\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~A sentence completion question about nuclear fission which discriminated well between students. 72 % of students scored 3 marks, 21% scored 2 marks and 5% scored 1 mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~99.5% of students scored the mark for writing down the correct equation. However, in this exam series all equations were provided for the students on the Physics Equations Sheet.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~A standard demand calculation of energy question. 48% of students scored 3 marks, while 41% of students scored 2 marks. Not converting or incorrectly converting the unit will have allowed many students to only score 2 marks.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\u8195?\~44% of students scored a mark. \u8216?Wear protective clothing\u8217? or \u8216?hazmat suits\u8217? were common incorrect answers. Any idea of \u8216?burying it\u8217? was credited, so \u8216?bury it in a field\u8217? was ok, as was \u8216?bury it in landfill,\u8217? which from a student\u8217?s perspective is equivalent to burying it underground. \u8216?Keeping it away from people\u8217? was insufficient.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (e)\u8195?\~96% of students scored 2 marks. The answer of 29.2 days was worth 1 mark as this could only have been calculated by doing the correct calculation and then subtracting the result from 365.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q3.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~47% of students correctly identified the particle.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~86% of students scored 2 marks on this calculation question.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~57% of students correctly identified the charges.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\u8195?\~75% of students correctly identified the description of the force.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (e)\u8195?\~78% of students identified the correct alpha particle.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (f)\u8195?\~ 39% of students answered correctly. \u8216?Tiny spheres\u8217? was enough to score the mark for students who could not manage to write all 6 words.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (g)\u8195?\~23% of students identified the correct model.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q4.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~29% of students scored 2 marks, while 27% of students scored 1 mark for this question about isotopes.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~51% of students knew what half-life means.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~77% of students correctly identified the least stable isoptope.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\u8195?\~Students found this question difficult with only 38% scoring 2 marks and 21% scoring 1 mark for this question about irradiation and contamination.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (e)\u8195?\~68% of students identified the purpose of the sticky floor.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (f)\u8195?\~ 72% of students scored 2 marks on this calculation question.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (g)\u8195?\~This question discriminated well with 46% of students scoring 2 marks and 20% of students scoring 1 mark for this question about fission and fusion\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q7.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~\~40% of student scored a mark on this question. Electronics, microwaves, phones and food and drink were common incorrect responses. Man-made doesn\u8217?t mean \u8216?made by man\u8217? in this context it refers to the radiation source being artificial rather than naturally occurring. Incorrect answers commonly seen included things which are not man-made, eg sunlight, cosmic rays, other types of radiation which do not contribute to background radiation like microwaves or infrared.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~\~74% of students could name a nuclear fuel. The most popular answer being \u8216?uranium\u8217?. Commonly seen incorrect answers included coal, oil, gas and petrol.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~\~Only 6% of students scored all 4 marks. To score the first marking point, students needed to indicate that the neutron becomes part of the U-235 nucleus, it was insufficient to only say that the neutron collides with the nucleus. Very few students scored the 4\fs20\super th\fs22\nosupersub  marking point. Many students hadn\u8217?t revised the process, evidenced by their meandering discussion of different radiation types and gold sheets. Whilst many students had some idea of the nuclear fission process, they often failed to gain marks because their answers lacked detail or were imprecise, eg neutrons being absorbed by \u8216?atoms\u8217? rather than \u8216?nuclei\u8217?, a nucleus \u8216?splitting\u8217? without the detail of two daughter nuclei being produced. A number of students went on to describe the chain reaction and / or the process of electricity generation which was unnecessary.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\~\~\~\~\~Only 8% of students scored both marks, with 29% of students scoring 1 mark. Elements fuse or atoms fuse was insufficient. Nuclei fuse was insufficient unless they said to form a larger / heavier nucleus. Very few knew that some of the mass of the nuclei was converted into energy. Some students fell back on their knowledge of chemical reactions and mentioned breaking bonds and exothermic reactions.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (e)\~\~\~\~\~Only 8% of students scored 2 marks, 39% of students scored 1 mark. Very few students linked the short half-life to how the activity of the waste would change with time. Most students who scored the first marking point did so for saying the radioactive waste would decay quicker. Students tended to score the second marking point rather than the first, although vague statements like less harm/damage to the environment were insufficient.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q8.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~More than 90% of students were able to gain at least one mark, usually for identifying gamma rays as the radiation released. Around one quarter scored all three marks.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Common incorrect answers were \u8216?a uranium neutron\u8217? along with \u8216?absorbs a proton\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~Around two thirds of students scored both marks. The value obtained by dividing the given values was 10.4 and many students thought about the context of the question and realised that therefore 11 power stations would be needed. The main error was rounding down to 10, which allowed a further 20% of students to score one mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 A few students multiplied the two numbers given to get an answer of 6 \'d7 10\fs20\super 13\fs22\nosupersub  power stations.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~Only 6% of students scored both marks, with a further 25% scoring one mark. The most common correct answer was a reference to radioactive waste. Some referred to the potential for a catastrophic accident to score a mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 Those who failed to score marks often referred to the waste as being \u8216?harmful\u8217? without specifying why this was the case. Many students seem to think that \u8216?harmful radiation\u8217? is given off all the time from a nuclear power station. There is also a common misconception, that nuclear power stations produce carbon dioxide.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\u8195?\~Just under half of students scored both marks by stating the similarity as the increase in both carbon dioxide concentration and global temperature and the difference in the pattern after the year 2000.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi0 A number of students failed to score marks because they selected spurious data, for example that they both graphs went from the year 1900 to past 2000 but had different numbers on the \i y\i0 -axis.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q9.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\u8195?\~11% of students scored 3 marks, 27% of students scored 2 marks. Lots of thorough answers were seen, but they needed to state the advantages of nuclear, rather than disadvantages of shale gas. \u8216?Shale gas releases CO\fs20\sub 2\fs22\nosupersub \u8217? did not score a mark, but \u8216?nuclear does not release CO\fs20\sub 2\fs22\nosupersub \u8217? did score a mark. Information about shale gas was given in the question, which is why these answers were insufficient for a mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\u8195?\~66% answered this question correctly. The most common correct answer was uranium. A common incorrect answer seen was oil.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\u8195?\~Many good answers seen with 12% scoring 4 marks and 20% scoring 3 marks. Some students didn\u8217?t mention the nucleus absorbing the neutron or the nucleus splitting in two, so they didn\u8217?t score the first two marking points. The first marking point was the least likely to score. The third marking point was scored by most students.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q11.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Few of the students correctly gave 'neutrons'. The most popular answer was 'protons'.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ There were many incorrect answers such as ' power stations ' with the students not stating that in most nuclear reactors the energy released was to heat water to eventually generate electricity. Many students simply wrote the word "electricity" and did not score the mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Again few students correctly identified particle X as 'a neutron'.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Half of the students scored zero. A common error was to show only one neutron released from each U-235 nucleus or to show only one fragment produced. Others students showed 2 neutrons released from the given U-235 nuclei but did not show any fragments. A small number of students who did this went on to draw further U-235 nuclei which then released neutrons. This gained both marks as it did show the start of a chain reaction.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q12.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Nearly half of the students failed to score a mark, with the other half split roughly equally between 1 and 2 marks. Many good answers were as per the mark scheme, with the term nebula quite often used for dust and gas. Incorrect responses included "rocks in the atmosphere / air", or dust on its own. Many students also did not refer to gravity being the attracting force. Many students incorrectly referred to fission. Some students referred to fusion, but this was not relevant to the question.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Just over a third of the students scored this mark. The most common error was to use "atom" for nuclei. Some students confused fusion and fission. This question was not attempted by a significant minority of the students.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ "Better / Improved Technology ", was the most frequent correct answer given. Quite a few answers confused telescopes with microscopes. Similarly satellites were often mentioned without any further indication how they might improve observations. Some students did not score the mark because they thought that the reason scientists had not detected them before was because they did not know what to look for or because light from these stars had only just reached us (in order to be observed).\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ "Other/different Planets", was the most common correct answer, scored by just over half of the students. It was strange to see that many students mentioned that the evidence might have proved there were other life forms in the universe as this didn't seem to be linked to the question.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Although the mark was scored by nearly half of all students, many correct answers were imprecise in their explanation of "proving / supporting ", the theory.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ Nearly one tenth of the students did not attempt this question. Of those that did less than a quarter scored the mark, as students failed to identify the key idea of "only". Too often the information in the stem of the question was fed back using slightly different words or simply the same words in a slightly different order.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q13.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Two thirds of students scored both marks. Those failing to score often confused which particle had the same number and which had different numbers or included incorrect references to electrons but more commonly referred to \u8216?elements\u8217? rather than \u8216?an element\u8217? or \u8216?atoms\u8217?. Few students scored the marks by referring to atomic number and mass number.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Nine tenths of students gained the mark on this question for identifying the atomic number.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~Similarly, nine tenths of students gained the mark on this question for correctly calculating the number of neutrons.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~More than half of students gained all three marks here. Poor arithmetic was as much to blame as poor understanding for those who made mistakes.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Three-quarters of students correctly identified the neutron. Incorrect answers included electron, proton, hydrogen, uranium and gamma.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~Half of students gained both marks, but nearly as many scored zero. The most common mistake was to not recognise the speed of the iron nuclei as one-tenth the speed of light resulting in their answer being incorrect by a factor of ten. Omitting the 0.1 gave a value of 0.00004 but often seen was some other power of 10 error - possibly due to calculator misuse. Occasionally students rearranged incorrectly and got a value of 2500. The use of standard form was not as common as hoped; approximately half the correct answers were not in standard form and many partially correct answers were expressed as a fraction.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iv)\~\~\~\~Three fifths of students scored zero marks on this question. Very few realised that a linear accelerator would need to be very long, while a circular accelerator could be built in a smaller space. Some students described acceleration in a circle and centripetal forces. Many students clearly did not understand what the question was asking.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Nearly all students gained at least one mark and three-quarters gained both. Some students failed to tick two boxes.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Two thirds of students gained both marks. Most students realised that the combined mass numbers and proton number should total 265 / 108. There were often unusual mass and atomic numbers written for the alpha particle; 0 & -1 were often seen, but also numbers containing figures to the right of a decimal point and numbers greater than 4.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (d)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Two thirds of students scored both marks for correct plotting and a suitable line of best fit. Only a small number of students did not draw a straight line. Some straight lines were drawn to far from the data and so did not score a mark. Mis-plotting could lead the student to draw a curve and these were accepted if they were drawn well.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Nine tenths of students gained the mark for correctly extrapolating the line and estimating a suitable number.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q14.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Just under half of the students scored this mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Just over three quarters of the students scored this mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c) \~\~\~\~This mark was scored by just over three quarters of the students.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q15.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Less than half of the students scored this mark. The most common error was to use colloquial terminology of \u8216?splitting the atom\u8217? rather than referring to the nucleus.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Just fewer than half of the students named the particle as a neutron to score this mark. Common errors were \u8216?alpha particles\u8217? or \u8216?Uranium 235\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Although this was quite accessible, many of the students simply quoted a rule like \u8216?like charges repel\u8217? without relating their answer to nuclei. Others did not interpret the question properly and attempted to explain about forces within the nucleus or between the nucleus and electrons. About half of the students scored this mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~This question was better answered with most of the students scoring the mark. The students that failed to score the mark either gave far too general answers like \u8216?in space\u8217? or focussed on other contexts \u8722? both biological and geological \u8722? with references to fertilisation and to volcanoes.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Many students had a good attempt at this question with almost half of the students scoring one mark and a further third scoring both. Many students gave an economic argument which was not creditworthy.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~This was poorly answered. Correct answers were not common, but referrals to reducing reliability on other fuel sources and reducing environmental damage were the most frequent. In suggesting an important consequence of developing fusion technology, many students expressed concern over the release of radioactive material, meltdown and references to the reactors running out of control. Incorrect responses like \u8216?difficult to dispose of the radioactive waste\u8217? or \u8216?lots of carbon dioxide emissions\u8217? were common.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d) \~\~\~\~Of those students that did attempt an approach which resembled a half-life calculation, those that identified there were three half-lives were awarded one mark and in most cases these students went on to correctly calculate the answer and score both marks. There were a significant number of students who identified the sequence 80-40-20-10 and then incorrectly thought this made four half-lives and another group that divided into 36 years to arrive at an answer of 4.5 years. Only one third of the students scored both marks.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q16.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Most of the students scored very well here, with nearly three quarters gaining both marks. \u8216?J\u8217? was usually seen with \u8216?it is the smallest\u8217?. Some students tried to elaborate further and ended up by confusing their answer. Another popular answer was \u8216?(only) small stars become Black Dwarfs\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Both \u8216?become a supernova\u8217? and \u8216?it will explode\u8217? were seen here although only one response was needed. About a quarter of the students scored zero and those that did often had either a subsequent incorrect stage written down or \u8216?it will implode\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~This was not done very well with over four fifths of the students scoring zero. Most of the students mentioned the lack of weighing machines or another method for measuring the mass. Another popular response was the idea that we cannot visit Betelgeuse.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~This was generally done very well with half of the students giving one of the responses involving advances in measuring, scientific knowledge, or technology. Unfortunately a few of the students continued with the idea of technology extending to the building of spaceships enabling people to visit Betelgeuse.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ This was usually done well, with two fifths of the students scoring 3 or more marks. Some of the students were very unsure of the different stages and just wrote down Red Giant and White Dwarf and this enabled them to obtain two marks. The last stage was a little confusing to some of the students as they wrote about the star losing all of its energy or running out of energy rather than the star stopping emitting energy.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q17.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Nearly three fifths of the students gave the correct answer, \u8216?number of protons\u8217?. Many of the students did not understand the term \u8216?in common\u8217? and instead, wrote about the differences between isotopes.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~About two fifths of the students correctly stated that nuclei are split in nuclear fission. Most of the remaining students had an idea of what happens but used ambiguous and vague terminology, using \u8216?break apart\u8217?, \u8216?divide\u8217? \u8216?particles\u8217? without supporting explanation and thus lacked sufficient clarity to obtain the mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~A lack of clarity again stopped students obtaining this mark with only about two fifths naming the reactor as the part where molybdenum is produced.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ About two thirds of the students identified the radiation as beta. However the reasons given were often confused, imprecise and sometimes contradictory. Examples seen include: \u8216?atomic number stays the same but number of protons goes up\u8217?, \u8216?nucleus loses a proton and gains a neutron\u8217?, \u8216?nucleus loses a neutron but gains a proton and an electron\u8217?, etc. Less than a third of the students gave complete answers that correctly gave the marking points in the mark scheme.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\~\~\~\~ Only less than a third of the students gave answers sufficient to score the mark. A small proportion of the students gave an answer in terms of the count rate halving.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (e)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~About two thirds of the students recognised that the number remaining was 20,000 but then less than half of these students used the graph to correctly identify 6.2-6.3 days as the time required. A small amount of students drew lines on the graph at 80,000 and identified 0.8 days but half of them, then carried out further calculations on this and consequently lost the compensation mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Fewer than a third of the students scored the mark for the ionising effect of radiation; of those who did, they usually went on to score the second mark. Most of the students that scored the second mark did so for general terms about radiation \u8216?causing cancer\u8217? or some form of harm. Few students linked the ionising effect of radiation to damage or harm to individual cells or DNA.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~Many of the students reiterated statements from part e(ii) about the dangers of radiation rather than answering the question asked. Students\u8217? phrasing of their response was often confused with only about a fifth being able to describe that the benefits outweighed the risks.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q18.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Nearly all students knew that nuclear fission takes place within a reactor and that nuclear fusion takes place within a star.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Less than half of the students could state a way in which fusion differs from fission. Many statements referred to atoms or elements instead of nuclei.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~A nuclear equation representing fission was given and students were asked to use the information in the equation to describe the process of fission.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi0 This was well answered, with just under half of the students gaining all four marks. Many statements relating to fission were seen which ignored the given equation. For example \u8216?two or three neutrons are released\u8217? when the equation clearly showed three.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Only a quarter of students could complete a nuclear equation depicting beta minus emission by adding subscripts for atomic number and a correct symbol for a beta particle.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi0 Many students are unclear concerning the symbol, subscript and superscript for a beta particle.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q19.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ A majority of students gave the correct answer. The most popular incorrect answer was plutonium.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Over half of the students scored zero with a significant minority not attempting the question. Most students got confused between nuclei splitting and atoms splitting and most students forgot about the neutrons that are released.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c) \~\~\~\~This was better answered with nearly half of students scoring all three marks. A common error was to interchange the position of the turbine and generator.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q20.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Nearly two thirds of students scored this mark with the main incorrect answer being uranium; a very few candidates gave thorium instead of plutonium.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~ Despite the instruction to the contrary, too many students simply described the fission process. Marks scored tended to be 0 or 3. A significant minority of students did not attempt the question. The most common error in the good attempts tended to be to replace the turbine with the generator, although propellers and rotators were also erroneously mentioned. Those students who answered successfully demonstrated a clear and detailed knowledge of the process, by describing the whole process, including the turbine driving the generator.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~The students who knew how to approach this diagram did well. Unfortunately a significant minority of students did not even attempt the diagram. The most frequent error was showing the process as continuing from the two daughter nuclei shown. Often candidates seemed to have an idea of the process but either failed to show it clearly, or did not use labels to support poor diagrams.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c) \~\~\~\~Most students who scored at least one mark did so by knowing neutrons were absorbed by the boron, although they often failed to make this absolutely clear. The descriptions for the third marking point too often failed to earn credit, by simply writing as a result \u8220?less energy released\u8221?.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q21.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Very few students gained this mark. Most responses did not have any reference to forces or that the forces would be balanced.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~This was well answered with the majority of the students being able to identify the relationship between the relative mass of the star and the estimated time.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~ This was poorly answered with many vague responses in terms of stars being \u8216?different\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ Just over half of the students gained one mark for correctly choosing \u8216?faster than\u8217?. However these students often went on to simply give a repetition of the question as the explanation. Few students referred explicitly to the time spent in the main sequence, instead using terms like \u8216?its life is shorter\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c) \~\~\~\~This was very poorly answered with a significant minority of students scoring zero. A small amount failed to attempt the question. The better students knew the stages that a large star passes through after the main sequence period but were often unable to give a description of what happens to a star. Simple statements such as \u8216?it expands to give a red supergiant\u8217? were sufficient to gain some credit. A significant number of students described the life cycle up to the main sequence period whilst others described the lifecycle of the Sun. The vast majority of the students seemed to simply put down everything they could think of in a totally random order. Many students were very creative, with new types of stars being named, black holes forming new stars and the complete life cycle being described in terms of a butterfly!\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q22.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Just over half of the students scored both marks. Some students failed to use the term \u8220?fusion\u8221? in respect of the conversion of hydrogen to helium.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~A small number of students scored both marks and nearly half scored one mark, usually for the \u8220?supernova\u8221?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c) \~\~\~\~Three-quarters of the students scored zero. There was little reference to evidence and most of the students merely said \u8220?the Big Bang\u8221?.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q23.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ The term \u8216?nuclear fission\u8217? was well known. However, it is alarming how many students think that nuclear fuels are burned in order to release energy. The process of generating electricity was not well described; many students seem to think that it is the turbine that generates the electricity. An advantage of nuclear fuels \u8216?reliability\u8217? was often given. However, many students spent a lot of time and filled a lot of space describing numerous disadvantages of nuclear energy and / or the advantages and disadvantages of wind with no reference at all to nuclear energy.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Nearly a fifth of students did not attempt this question. Many students simply repeated the stem of the question and had no idea about the storage of the carbon dioxide following its \u8216?capture\u8217?. A common error was the assumption that \u8216?carbon capture\u8217? involves the removal of the existing carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q24.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Only 8% of students could explain the difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion. Only a further 7% could describe one of the processes correctly. The majority of answers simply stated that fusion was natural and fission happens in power stations.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~There were a large number of different answers given, unfortunately few were correct. Approximately 37% of students did gain a mark, generally for either energy or heat.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~\~Just over 20% of students failed to attempt this question despite it being a straight recall of information from the specification. Approximately 35% of students gave a correct answer.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Only 22% of students gained this mark. The most common error was to state that the number of neutrons would be the same.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q25.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~\~(i)\~\~\~\~\~ Nearly two-thirds of students scored the maximum of three marks by correctly sequencing the three stages of one type of star. A common error was to include \u8216?black hole\u8217? in the sequence.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Over two-thirds of students chose the correct star. However, there were few correct reasons given for their choice, many students simply restating the similarity of the size of Alpha Centauri A and the Sun from the table.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Only a third of students could correctly identify the process by which energy is given out in stars.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q26.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 \~(a)\~\~\~\~ Many students were able to recall that main sequence stars undergo nuclear fusion, although there were a number of students who thought that protostars also undergo fusion, but at a slower rate. The spelling of fusion was not always clear, with some students putting a dot / dots above what may have been the letter \u8216?u\u8217? or \u8216?ii\u8217?; this was taken as being fission and so did not score a mark. Many students were aware that protostars are not giving off energy / radiation whilst main sequence stars are. A small number of students attempted to describe the life cycle of a star. \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Surprisingly, less than two-thirds of students knew fusion was involved. Many students had hydrogen fusing to form the heavier elements, rather than helium. Few students recalled that elements heavier than iron are formed in a supernova. A significant number of responses allocated a role to the planets and made reference to the \u8216?Big Bang\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q27.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 This question demonstrated the need for candidates to learn basic facts. Just over a quarter of candidates scored zero and a further two\'2dfifths scored only one mark. There was a large variety of incorrect links, one of the most common being a chain reaction happening in a star.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q28.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Just under three-quarters of candidates scored this mark, with most answers given in terms of gravitation.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~There were two alternative ways to answer this part question. Even so, only a fifth of candidates managed to score both marks. Some candidates did not refer to \u8216?radiation pressure\u8217? correctly although the idea was inherent in their response. Some candidates did not include the direction of the forces involved. There were a significant number of candidates who lost marks by references to \u8216?burning\u8217? hydrogen / fuel.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~\~(i)\~\~\~\~\~ Just over half of the candidates scored one mark. However, some lost marks by references to \u8216?burning\u8217? and others to \u8216?fission\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Few candidates scored this mark. There were few references to the amount of hydrogen in a star.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d) \~\~\~\~Again this question was poorly answered with only two\'2dfifths of candidates scoring the mark. It was evident that some candidates do not take notice of the space and mark allocation given to questions and gave extensive, usually incorrect, responses to this question.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q29.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Just over a quarter of candidates gained full marks by either commenting on the mass number and atomic number or identifying the neutron decaying to a proton and electron. A number of candidates wrongly thought the proton number decreased and confused the atomic number with the mass number, although a further fifth of candidates were able to gain two marks. A further quarter of candidates scored one mark for correctly identifying beta, without being able to explain why. A number of candidates incorrectly identified the radiation but then went on to give a correct explanation for beta radiation.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q30.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Considering how often this or a similar question has been asked it is surprising that few candidates gained all three marks. Weaker candidates gave vague responses such as\u8216?forces are equal\u8217? without being specific about the type of forces or their direction. Some candidates took the alternative route of saying that there is sufficient hydrogen available to keep the fusion reactions going for a long time but there was rarely a reference to this happening at a continuous/steady rate.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Most candidates correctly identified the opinion as being\u8216?that the Sun will remain stable for several billion years into the future\u8217?, but then failed to give the idea of evidence or correct usage of the facts given in the passage to suggest how scientists could justify this.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q31.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~This was not answered well. The principal errors were candidates responding in terms of atoms, rather than nuclei, and descriptions based on fission processes.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Few candidates were aware of the site of nuclear fusion reactions, with common misconceptions placing this process within volcanoes or the atmosphere.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~\~(i)\~\~\~\~\~ Just under half of the candidates were able to state why nuclear fusion reactors are not used to produce electricity from the information given. Many incorrect responses were in terms of vague statements involving doubts about the safety aspects of nuclear power in general.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Unfortunately, most candidates suggested that the \b sole\b0  reason for the continuing search for sustainable nuclear fusion in a reactor was due to issues involving fossil fuels and carbon dioxide and did not receive credit.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q32.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ A number of candidates realised the importance of gravity but fewer candidates used the term \u8216?radiation pressure\u8217? to score both points.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Most candidates stated that nuclear fusion is the process by which other elements are formed and some candidates were able to give hydrogen to helium and other \u8216?light\u8217? elements as examples. However, only a small minority of candidates were able to indicate the conditions required to form \u8216?heavy\u8217? elements. Some candidates confused their answers by referring to chemical reactions.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q33.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ This mark was scored by over three quarters of candidates, the common incorrect answers were \u8216?isomers\u8217?, \u8216?allotropes\u8217? and ions.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~There were many correct answers, with relatively few candidates getting only one of the numbers correct.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~\~(i)\~\~\~\~\~ There was the usual illegible and poor spelling of \u8216?fission\u8217? where it was difficult to see if the candidate had written a u or an i. Many of the candidates gained the mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~\~Only just over half of candidates were able to recall this, with nucleus and electron being common incorrect answers.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d) \~\~\~\~Just over half of candidates were able to correctly identify plutonium, although the spelling was often incorrect. Polonium was a common incorrect answer along with potassium. The \u8216?acceptable\u8217? answer of MOX was rarely seen.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q34.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Most candidates gave at least one of the correct responses although some lost credit by listing contradictory points. Some candidates gave the full life story of a star which necessitated the use of additional pages even though they had gained the marks in the first sentence. \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b) \~\~\~\~Only a minority of candidates gained the two marks by recognising radiation pressure as one of the factors involved. A significant number of candidates gave correct responses in terms of fusion although a minority of candidates gave answers involving either fission or the burning of hydrogen.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c) \~\~\~\~As in part (a), candidates gave very long responses requiring extra sheets or answers out of the clip area; in some of these longer responses, candidates contradicted themselves. Most candidates gained one mark for \u8216?supernova\u8217? but very few gave a correct explanation of what happens next.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q35.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 This question was accessible to all candidates with most showing a good understanding of these key processes. The majority of candidates scored three or four marks.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q36.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Many candidates \u8216?confused\u8217? chemical reactions with nuclear reactions. They should note that there are nuclear fusion reactions and nuclear fission reactions. Candidates who did not clearly refer to the correct term did not gain credit.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q37.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Foundation Tier\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ The relative mass and relative charge of the subatomic particles was not well known by most candidates with few scoring both marks. Some candidates clearly did not understand the question giving answers of \u8216?\u8211?1\u8217? or \u8216?2\u8217? for relative mass.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ This question was poorly answered. Very few candidates scored both marks. In most cases, where one mark was scored, candidates were able to get over the idea of positive cancelling negative in some way but very few gained the mark for stating that the number of protons and electrons was equal.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ The variety of answers given meant that the vast majority of candidates did not score this mark. Many candidates only said that uranium-235 or uranium-238 contained a different number of neutrons rather than being more specific and stating that uranium-235 had fewer neutrons.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Few candidates knew which subatomic particle caused the splitting of atoms in a nuclear reactor. The variety of answers seemed to indicate a lot of guesswork taking place.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ Very few candidates knew the correct scientific name for the process. Many candidates named chemical processes and nearly 20% of all candidates left the answer space blank.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ \b Higher Tier\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Most candidates scored both marks. However some candidates indicated they were not really understanding the question by putting \u8216?-1\u8217? for relative mass.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Few candidates scored both marks. Most scored one mark for suggesting that the charges cancelled but few mentioned that the numbers of electrons and protons was the same.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ The majority of candidates did not know the correct answer and tended to state that there were \u8216?different numbers of protons or/and neutrons\u8217?\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Almost half of the candidates scored this mark. Popular incorrect answers included \u8216?nucleus\u8217? and \u8216?alpha particle\u8217?\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ Most candidates scored this mark. The candidates who did not, often cited chemical processes such as diffusion, electrolysis, cracking or the Haber process.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q38.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ A large majority of candidates identified the force as gravitational.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ A small minority of candidates were able to offer a correct response with \u8216?bigger stars\u8217? as a popular incorrect answer.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ A majority of candidates were able to offer a correct response.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ A majority of candidates scored the mark but it is of concern that some candidates suggested universe, Earth or moon as possible names for our galaxy.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q39.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)(ii)\~ Most candidates were able to express correctly the relationships between gravity and the mass of the dust and gas and between gravity and the distance apart of the dust and gas.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ About half the candidates scored at least one mark as credit was given for an explanation of why the star remains stable or why this period lasts for millions of years. Two marks were given for a response which correctly refers to both aspects, eg \u8216?the forces acting inside the star are balanced and the star has a vast supply of hydrogen\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ Just over a third of candidates obtained at least one mark. However, some candidates demonstrated their lack of understanding by suggesting that, in stage 4, nuclear reactions start or that a star is formed.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q40.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ This was usually correct, and correctly spelt.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ This was quite well done, with many candidates finding it easier to show diagrammatically what was happening. A large number referred to uranium \i atoms rather than nuclei.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\i0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ Less than 50% of candidates scored this mark. There were many vague \u8216?it goes up\u8217? responses.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ A number of candidates did not attempt this question. However many candidates were able to apply their knowledge of a chain reaction and the information given in the question to gain full marks. A common erroneous answer was in terms of an increase in gas pressure due to lowering the rods\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q41.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Nearly all candidates recognised that the reference at the end of the passage is to a supernova, though some suggested the big bang.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Most responses were disappointing and did not gain any marks. Few candidates answered in terms of elements and consequently even fewer explained that atoms of the heaviest elements can only be formed by nuclear fusion at the very high temperatures of a supernova.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q42.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ The majority of candidates gained at least 1 mark.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Just under a third of candidates knew that stars are able to give out energy for millions of years by atoms joining together.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ Three quarters of candidates correctly identified \u8216?The Milky Way\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (d)\~\~\~\~ Just over a third of candidates selected the correct reason.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q43.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 A significant proportion of candidates seemed to think that the answer to at least one part of a question on this subject must be \u8216?big bang\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ This question was generally well answered and though fusion was often spelled with a double \u8216?s\u8217? there were few obvious fudges between \u8216?fusion\u8217? and \u8216?fission\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Just under half of candidates failed to answer the question and instead gave more details of the process of nuclear fusion; however the most popular incorrect response was \u8216?big bang\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (iii)\~\~\~\~ There was a surprising range of answers to this item, with \u8216?gravity\u8217? and \u8216?big bang\u8217? being the most common wrong answers.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ Few candidates were confident that this statement is supported by the available evidence or is not contradicted by the available evidence or that the available evidence extrapolates to this idea. Many candidates simply wrote down something they thought they knew about the universe.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q44.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Foundation Tier\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ Most answers were in terms of only gas being involved in some massive explosion. A significant number of candidates stated that the stars were formed from exploding planets. Only a small number of candidates mentioned gravity pulling dust and gas together and very few mentioned nuclear fusion.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ \b Higher Tier\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ This question was very well answered by most candidates.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ A good number of candidates gave a correct explanation of how stars like the Sun are formed. Common errors included \u8216?Big Bang\u8217?, \u8216?rocks\u8217? or \u8216?burning gases\u8217?, and confusion between fusion and fission.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q45.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ This part was generally not well answered. Any attempts were flawed by inaccuracies and vagueness. It was common to think that \u8216?particles\u8217? and \u8216?electrons\u8217? initiated the process, and that they were often incident on \u8216?atoms\u8217?. Diagrams were often messy, and rarely adequately labelled.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ The mark for this part was mostly given for better explanations of safety implications; most candidates gave answers that could equally apply to any power station.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (c)\~\~\~\~ Many candidates used the table to calculate how many times more energy was released from uranium than from coal, and many candidates gave statements that were true but which made no reference to any benefit to the environment. A significant number of candidates seemed to think that uranium is burnt in a nuclear power station.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q46.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ A number of candidates confused the way stars produce energy with the way in which stars are formed. There was more confusion between \u8216?fusion\u8217? and \u8216?fission\u8217?, but many knew that hydrogen and helium were involved. Less able candidates talked about stars burning (often hydrogen) or reflecting light. Some candidates had stars reflecting light from the Sun.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (b)\~\~\~\~ This part was very poorly answered; very few candidates knew that the presence of heavier elements was the evidence required.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (c)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Many candidates could describe the black hole correctly, although the less able candidates talked about things being \u8216?sucked in\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Hardly any candidates knew that X-rays were produced as the gases from a star spiralled into the black hole.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q47.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Part (a) was answered well by the more able candidates, but many less able candidates were very vague and mentioned protons, neutrons and electrons as if they were all equally relevant. Answers to part (b)(i ) often did not refer to the graph whilst answers from many candidates to part (b)(ii) showed poor graph reading skills, with the weaker candidates subtracting values rather than dividing them. Part (c) was poorly answered by many candidates. Those candidates who knew what the question was referring to were rarely specific enough to gain full marks.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q48.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 In part (a) the process was rarely named, most candidates knew the process generated energy but many talked of the helium giving energy to the Sun. Descriptions in part (b) varied from precise to completely wrong. Sequencing was not well understood, and the weaker candidates used coloured giants and dwarfs indiscriminately.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q51.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 In (i) there were many correct answers, the common error being to fuse atoms rather than nucleii. A number of candidates described fission rather than fusion. In part (ii) some candidates gained both marks but many just gained one mark for the mass reduction as fusion occurs; they failed to complete the answers by saying the mass loss was converted into energy. A minority wrote about fusion as an exothermic chemical reaction. \par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q52.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 In part (a) only the most able candidates were able to identify the two forces acting on the Sun correctly, though more of them realised that the forces are balanced. A number of candidates referred to the stability of the Solar System. \par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ The answers to part (b) varied in quality form those where candidates were fortunate to pick up marks by mentioning \u8216?red giant\u8217?, \u8216?white dwarf\u8217? etc. amongst a lot of mis-ordered or irrelevant information; to those which covered the whole range of possibilities in a well-structured order and could have gained ten marks if these had been available. For the really able candidates, or the candidate with a genuine interest in Astronomy, this must have been a satisfying way to end the examination.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q53.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The majority of candidates seemed unaware of the account of a star\u8217?s lifetime as given in the Specification. Most candidates referred to red giants and white dwarfs but only the best could explain these in terms of forces.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q54.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 The \u8216?star story\u8217? continues to present problems for the majority of candidates.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (a)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Only the most able stated that a supernova was involved or that large gravitational forces were involved. Many answered in terms of \u8216?light being sucked in\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-567 (ii)\~\~\~\~ Few appeared to know about the detection of X-rays produced when gases spiral into a black hole. The majority stated that they can be detected \u8216?because there is nothing there\u8217?.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1701\ri567\fi-1134 (b)\~\~\~\~ (i)\~\~\~\~\~ Many answered in terms of dust particles joining together, often due to gravity. Only the best candidates answered in terms of fusion of the nuclei of the lighter elements.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q55.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Responses were generally disappointing. Too many candidates simply restated information from the stem and then went on to describe the sequence of events once the main stable period had ended. Many candidates could describe how \u8216?dust and gas\u8217? were pulled together by gravity, but then wrote very vague explanations of what happened next. A minority of candidates realised that the process that released energy was called \u8216?nuclear fusion\u8217?. There were as many candidates who thought this energy was from the burning of hydrogen. Very few candidates understood the idea that during the main stable period of the Sun the gravitational pull is balanced by expansion forces, caused by the very high temperatures.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q56.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 There were mostly poor responses to all parts of this question. The sequence of the life cycle of a star was given to candidates in diagrammatic form and many candidates were still unable to describe the sequence in the correct order.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q57.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 Few candidates got this question correct. A very small minority gave excellent answers.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45 Q58.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-567 (a)\~\~\~\~ Many candidates correctly used the figures to demonstrate a (very slight) loss of mass during the fusion reaction.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li1134\ri567\fi-1134 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ (b)\~\~\~\~ Relatively few candidates \ul related\ul0  the loss of mass form part (a) and the huge energy output referred to in the stem of part (b) by pointing out that mass \u8594? energy with a huge release of energy (as compared to chemical reactions involving the same number of atoms). Though this was recalled knowledge, the recall was strongly cued.\par
\f1\fs27\cf1\b\ltrpar\ql\sb450\sa45\li150\ri45\fi0 Q59.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\b0\ltrpar\ql\sb0\sa0\li567\ri567 (a) In view of the regrettable misprint in the paper, full credit was also given in this item for correct use of the formula energy released = mass x velocity.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ Nevertheless, fully correct answers were seldom seen, most candidates failing to scale correctly both by 0.007 (for the %) and by 0.001 (for g \f3 \'ae\f1  kg).\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ In (b)(i) most candidates recognised the inverse relationship between a star\u8217?s mass and its lifetime on the main sequence, despite it being counter-intuitive. Many, however, regarded the relationship as being inversely proportional whereas the lifetime is reduced out of all proportion to the increase in mass.\par
\f1\fs22\cf0\ltrpar\ql\sb240\sa0\li567\ri567\fi-567 \~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~\~ In (b)(ii) though a significant minority of candidates correctly referred to the increased rate at which more massive stars used up their hydrogen, fewer related this to the increased temperature and pressure in the core and fewer still that this effect must be more than proportional to the star\u8217?s mass.\par
}